WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.200
Hello! Welcome to the long-awaited bounty vlog&nbsp;
number one about my challenge of 100 hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.520
hunting on a public program from Hackerone. In&nbsp;
this video, I will show you what program was that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.680
how did I approach it, what did I report and,&nbsp;
finally, how much I earned. I will also tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.160
where to find my methodology and my notes. Enjoy!&nbsp;
For those of you who didn't watch the bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.400
vlog number 0, I will just briefly talk about my&nbsp;
background. So for three years I was a pentester&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.800
and I would say I have quite solid background in&nbsp;
web security. However, when it came to bug bounty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.120
I was jumping from one program to another&nbsp;
and I wasn't finding many bugs. I only had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.120
one monetary reward which is also covered&nbsp;
in a video on this channel. So up until now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.360
I wasn't a very successful bughunter. When I was&nbsp;
recording a podcast with David Shutz, he said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.160
many smart things but one of them was that when he&nbsp;
understands the flow, the bugs come naturally. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:11.840
I decided to test on myself if what's being said&nbsp;
in my podcast works for me or not. So I decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:20.160
to spend 100 hours on a bug bounty program. But&nbsp;
importantly I didn't start it with attitude to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.960
find bugs. I started it with only wanting to&nbsp;
understand different flows in the application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:33.280
hoping that the bugs will come naturally to me&nbsp;
like they do to David. The rules were also that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.480
I don't want to do any subdomain enumeration or&nbsp;
directory brute force because I just wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:46.000
test if I have to do this or not. The program I&nbsp;
chose was Stripe. It's a payment provider that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.280
use myself to handle payments for BBRE Premium.&nbsp;
It's mostly an API with which we, as sellers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.560
integrate. But there's also a dashboard and&nbsp;
the scope also includes many other assets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.160
My first session on Stripe was 3,5 hours and&nbsp;
I focused on the flow that BBRE subscribers go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:12.080
through when subscribing to my paid newsletter.&nbsp;
And after this time, I understood what are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.560
requests, what are the parameters and so on but I&nbsp;
had no vulnerability. After that, I had to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.560
a few days on creating a video so I didn't&nbsp;
hack. But I consciously, throughout the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.800
thought about what could I do with this flow.&nbsp;
And the idea that came to my mind was to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:39.360
archived prices. Because at first, I was selling&nbsp;
BBRE Premium for $48 and after a week, I archived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:47.600
that old price and created a new of $79. So&nbsp;
if someone would be able to use the old price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:55.280
it would be saving of $31 so it's quite a good&nbsp;
impact. So I tried to use the old checkout link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:02.960
but it didn't work. Instead, I started the flow&nbsp;
with the new link but in the middle of the flow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:09.200
I changed back to the previous one and all of&nbsp;
a sudden, I was able to purchase BBRE Premium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:17.200
for $48. So I filed a report. It was my first&nbsp;
report on Hackerone since about two years so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:24.800
was an insane time and I had, what STOK calls, a&nbsp;
bug fever. I even turned on mobile notifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.960
for gmail in my phone to not miss any updates. In&nbsp;
general, it was a terrible experience because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.760
was getting loads of notifications and every time&nbsp;
something on gmail popped up, my heart started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:45.120
beating quicker. The bug was triaged as medium&nbsp;
which was very fair. For medium on api.stripe.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:52.640
the range is $1,000 - $5,000. Unfortunately, I was&nbsp;
at the bottom of the range but I was still very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.480
happy with this bounty. Especially considering&nbsp;
how much time I spent. Then, after the next hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.440
I was understanding more and more flows of Stripe&nbsp;
but I didn't have any bugs. So I wanted to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.760
elsewhere. In scope of bug bounty program there&nbsp;
are also repositories on Stripe account on GitHub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.080
Among others, there are SDKs to communicate with&nbsp;
Stripe API from different programming languages or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.720
from the terminal. I found a bug there after about&nbsp;
two days of hunting. It was very hard to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.040
exploit but it was a clear bug so I decided to&nbsp;
report it, even though I wasn't sure if they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.360
accept it or not. This one is not disclosed so I&nbsp;
can't really tell you more about it. But if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:44.240
are a member of BBRE Premium, you actually know&nbsp;
what method did I use to find this bug because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:50.000
I can't tell you "this is the tool that I used&nbsp;
to find this bug" because it's confidential but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.400
I can easily tell you, among tens and hundreds&nbsp;
of other tips in BBRE Premium, "this is how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:03.280
use this tool" and I know that it's the method&nbsp;
to exploit this bug but you don't know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:09.600
but if you read everything carefully, you know the&nbsp;
methods that I use. So this is what I do on BBRE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:16.080
Premium. So read everything carefully and after&nbsp;
a few months, maybe after disclosure, you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.640
realize that the article you read half a year ago&nbsp;
was the method that I used to exploit this bug.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:29.520
Coming back to the bug, it was rewarded $500&nbsp;
as a low which I agree with, which was on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:37.600
other hand, top of the range for this asset. So at&nbsp;
this point things were looking really really good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:46.080
much better than I expected because after about&nbsp;
24 hours of hunting, I earned $1600 with retests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:53.520
But then came the hardest part. I wasn't finding&nbsp;
anything throughout the next hours. I was spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:06:00.480
most of my time on stripe.com but I also looked&nbsp;
at other assets. After about 45 hours, I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:07.680
seriously considering ending the experiment after&nbsp;
50 hours, but I stuck with it. After 60 hours, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:14.080
was pretty much sure I won't find anything anymore&nbsp;
but I just wanted to complete that for the video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.600
I was really unmotivated and there was a lot of&nbsp;
procrastination which is one of the reasons why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:27.040
it took me so long to complete this 100 hours.&nbsp;
But then, after 82 hours, I found another bug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:32.880
which later turned out to be my biggest payout.&nbsp;
It was another open source application from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.160
GitHub and it's also pending disclosure&nbsp;
so I can't tell you more details about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:45.280
It was classified as low with which I don't&nbsp;
quite agree but apart from $500 of base payout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:51.680
I also got $1,000 of bonus because they were&nbsp;
happy that I'm attacking this particular asset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:59.920
for a total of $1,500. Most of the remaining time&nbsp;
I spent trying to find a bypass for this last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:09.520
vulnerability but I didn't find anything. So after&nbsp;
100 hours the final result is $3,100. But is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:17.040
really final? Maybe yes but maybe not. Because the&nbsp;
second bug - they tried to fix it but they didn't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.080
And it wasn't even a bypass - the original&nbsp;
proof of concept I presented still worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.600
after the attempt to fix it. So I mentioned&nbsp;
it in a retest, I mentioned it in comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:33.920
but there was no action from their side. So after&nbsp;
exactly 90 days from the original submission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:41.120
I decided to report it again to maybe get the&nbsp;
same bounty twice. And as I said previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:47.760
if they would close it as informational the first&nbsp;
time around, I wouldn't object. But for - me maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.680
my understanding is wrong, you can write&nbsp;
in comments what do you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.480
when you triage, pay and resolve the report&nbsp;
you kinda commit to fixing it. So the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:03.760
report was closed as duplicate but I opened&nbsp;
mediation and I don't know yet how it will end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.560
And another thing is that for this video, I&nbsp;
am also giving you my notes - more about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.320
later. But before disclosing them, I wanted&nbsp;
to contact Stripe if they are okay with me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:22.880
publishing this information because among typical&nbsp;
notes, there were also things that I call "quirks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:29.760
or potential vulnerabilities" which weren't risky&nbsp;
enough for me to report it but I hoped that maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:35.840
I will be able to chain them one day. For example,&nbsp;
there was a patg traversal in stripe-cli or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:42.320
XSSes without content security policy bypass.&nbsp;
All they told me back is that if I disclose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:48.320
something that turns out to be a vulnerability,&nbsp;
then I will not be paid for it. It didn't change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.840
anything because if I wanted to submit any&nbsp;
of this before I would do it long time ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:03.280
But one useful thing they told me is that they do&nbsp;
accept XSSes without CSP bypass. And I had two of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:09.600
them. They were on in-scope domain but they were&nbsp;
really hard to actually exploit to do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.520
malicious. So I didn't really want to report&nbsp;
this but I decided that I report one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:21.360
and if they accept this, I will consider reporting&nbsp;
the second one. But I don't think they will accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:28.240
this submission. I can already tell you that there&nbsp;
will be a second part of this bounty vlog. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:32.720
those reports are closed, I will tell you&nbsp;
more about them, I will show you my notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.640
and I will answer your questions you can submit&nbsp;
them in the comments down below. But the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:44.480
part will be a live stream on YouTube so you will&nbsp;
be also able to ask me questions in the real time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:49.840
on the chat. But don't ask me when will&nbsp;
it be because it doesn't depend on me.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.920
So let's think about if this $3,100 is a lot or&nbsp;
not. But before doing, that let's clear up what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:06.160
does the 100 hours number mean for me. Because&nbsp;
many of you probably think that it's 8 hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:14.560
work day times 12.5. So two and a half weeks of&nbsp;
full-time work. But it's not. Instead, I log only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:21.520
the real time of working and from a typical eight&nbsp;
hour work day, excluding breaks, useless meetings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:29.520
responding to emails, procrastination and so on,&nbsp;
you probably get 5-6 hours on a good day. For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:36.080
100 hours means about a month. Of course, I also&nbsp;
do videos, the newsletter and so on, and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:42.400
So for me it was spread across three months but&nbsp;
if I was only doing bug bounty, it would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:49.520
one month to complete 100 hours of hunting. Is it&nbsp;
as much as I would earn as a pentester? No, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:55.680
even in Poland. But I'm still very happy with this&nbsp;
number because at the beginning, I didn't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.840
this experiment at all. I thought I will find&nbsp;
nothing. So this number, on the other hand, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:08.320
something that I can easily live for for a month&nbsp;
in Kraków. Some of the factors that motivated me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:14.800
to quit my job is that I'm young, I don't have&nbsp;
kids, debts and inflation of living standards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:21.840
hasn't hit me badly, at least not yet. So I don't&nbsp;
spend that much. On the other hand, Stripe is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:27.600
well-paying program and those are only two lows&nbsp;
and one medium when you think about it. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:34.000
are a lot of what-ifs because those bugs had the&nbsp;
impact but also there were a lot of prerequisites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:40.160
for the attacks to be possible. So if some of&nbsp;
those prerequisites were not there or maybe if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:46.160
would get the middle of the range with the medium&nbsp;
risk bug, the payout would be much higher. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:52.640
also, with only those three submissions, I made&nbsp;
it to top 7 on the leaderboard and in hacktivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:58.560
you can see that no one apart from me in this&nbsp;
time submitted more than one report to Stripe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:06.240
The important takeaway for me is the type of&nbsp;
assets that I attack. Because out of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:14.640
100 hours I spent majority - maybe 60 hours - on&nbsp;
Stripe.com and I only found one bug after 4 hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:22.880
and then nothing after 56 hours. I had many&nbsp;
potential bugs, something that I could chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:29.680
and I hope that magically, something that I found&nbsp;
after 10 hours, I will be able to chain or turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:37.520
into a bug after 50 hours with something else.&nbsp;
But this moment never came. It taught me that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:44.320
though Stripe is very big, it's not as complex&nbsp;
as, let's say Google. Because, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:49.680
when you have one YouTube video, you can watch&nbsp;
it on YouTube, you can watch it on YouTube TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:56.000
you can see it from YouTube studio and then you&nbsp;
can also access it from Google ads. On Stripe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:03.440
I found very few places where the same resource&nbsp;
was modified from different components, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.000
different parts of the application and I think&nbsp;
it made hunting harder. And, of course, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:16.480
no question about the fact that I simply missed&nbsp;
a lot of bugs, as we all do. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:22.480
when I look at the time invested into open source&nbsp;
assets, it was well worth it and I think in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:29.280
future, I should focus more on that. Now if you&nbsp;
believe you will find bugs that I missed or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:36.320
just want to see how I organize my notes, check&nbsp;
out my whole Notion page with notes that I took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:42.560
throughout this 100 hours. Of course, there are&nbsp;
some confidential parts that I had to redact but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:48.800
majority is still there. You can get them if&nbsp;
you sign up for BBRE newsletter but I promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:55.520
you you won't regret this. The link is in the card&nbsp;
right now or in the description. If you want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:01.840
even a step further and learn with me in BBRE&nbsp;
Premium, check out premium.bugbountyxplained.com.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:08.480
I think it will be quite interesting after&nbsp;
these bugs get disclosed, I will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:14.400
to point you that the tip that led to this bug&nbsp;
was published in this issue of the newsletter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

